# Erdős Problem #99 — chain-cover sharpening of the atomic train gap to 0.00047

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Repository base:** `81b1eecc`
**Target:** Target C-II canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Claim class:** proof-only sharpening of audited-working §168
**External input:** none beyond the already-used König theorem / chain-graph argument
**Computation:** exact rational interval certificate for the final constants

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

Audited-working §168 proves the all-regime atomic train gap with `0.0004`; the current
AG42 proof-only refinement raises it to `0.00042`.  The quantitative bottleneck in
§168.3 is the phase-inversion deletion.  That deletion was bounded by a Markov
threshold, even though the inversion graph is a chain graph.  The sparse-chain cover
lemma already used in the canonical full-core endpoint exclusion gives a strictly
better loss.

The result is

\[
\boxed{
 x\sigma\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00047,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
}
\tag{AG47.1}
\]

for every §142 Type-II train-measure subsequential limit.  Consequently all uses of the
atomic gain may take

\[
\boxed{\eta_{grid}=0.00047}
\tag{AG47.2}
\]

at the same proof-only scope.

## 1. Dominant-orbit reduction

Put

\[
s_2=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
\eta=0.00047,
\qquad
\bar A=2s_2+\eta.
\]

Assume for contradiction that the total absolute atomic train mass satisfies

\[
A=x\sigma\le\bar A.
\]

Exactly as in §168.1, a dominant three-direction projective orbit has mass at least

\[
M_0={4s_2^2\over\bar A},
\]

and the total atomic mass outside it is at most

\[
R_0=\bar A-M_0.
\]

The dominant-orbit Mantel deficit is at most

\[
d_0={\bar A^2\over4}-s_2^2+{R_0^2\over4}.
\tag{AG47.3}
\]

The maximum-degree Mantel partition and majority assignment from §168.2 therefore leave
assigned atom mass at least

\[
S_0={M_0\over2}-\sqrt{d_0}-\sqrt{6d_0}
\tag{AG47.4}
\]

on each shore, while the number of missing cross-shore pairs is at most

\[
(2d_0+o(1))D^2.
\tag{AG47.5}
\]

As before, because at most three atom windows are present and both shores have positive
mass, choose opposite-shore atom families `A_D,B_D` with normalized masses at least

\[
S_0,
\qquad
S_0/2,
\tag{AG47.6}
\]

in some order.

Put

\[
p_0:=\sqrt{2d_0}.
\tag{AG47.7}
\]

## 2. The inversion graph is a chain graph

Choose real projective lifts centered at axes differing by `pi/3`, and write

\[
x_u=\alpha_u,
\qquad
y_v=\beta_v-\pi/3.
\]

Every actual strict-rhombus crossing has `y_v>x_u`.  Therefore every inversion

\[
x_u\ge y_v
\]

is a missing cross pair.

Let `H_D` be the bipartite graph of inversions.  It is a chain graph: after ordering
`A_D` by increasing `x_u`, the neighborhoods

\[
N_H(u)=\{v:y_v\le x_u\}
\]

are nested.

The endpoint-exclusion chain-cover lemma applies verbatim.  If a chain graph has `m`
edges, then its maximum matching has size `nu` with

\[
m\ge1+2+\cdots+\nu,
\]

so König's theorem gives a vertex cover of size at most `sqrt(2m)`.  Here

\[
m\le(2d_0+o(1))D^2,
\]

hence all inversions can be removed by deleting in total at most

\[
\boxed{
(2\sqrt{d_0}+o(1))D
=(\sqrt2\,p_0+o(1))D
}
\tag{AG47.8}
\]

trains across the two shores combined.

This replaces the old separate phase-pruning losses `p_0D` on each shore.  The old
total was `2p_0D`; the new total is at most `sqrt(2)p_0D`.

After deleting the cover, every retained pair has strict forward phase order.

## 3. Degree pruning and common-neighbor threshold

The total missing-pair count is still at most `(2d_0+o(1))D^2=p_0^2D^2+o(D^2)`.
Delete from each shore every train missing more than `(p_0+o(1))D` opposite trains.
Markov removes at most

\[
(p_0+o(1))D
\]

trains from each shore.  Every retained train then misses at most that many opposite
trains.

In the worst distribution of the chain-cover deletions, the smaller selected family
has normalized size at least

\[
{S_0\over2}-(\sqrt2+1)p_0-o(1).
\]

Thus every triple on the opposite shore has a common neighbor provided

\[
{S_0\over2}>(4+\sqrt2)p_0.
\tag{AG47.9}
\]

The exact interval certificate verifies a much stronger margin:

\[
{S_0\over2}-(4+\sqrt2)p_0
>0.27749.
\tag{AG47.10}
\]

Hence the §163 actual-`K_{2,2}` consistency argument again produces one global crossing
order on each retained family.

## 4. Opposite-corner path with the reduced loss

The two selected families start with total normalized mass at least `3S_0/2`.
Phase deletion loses at most `sqrt(2)p_0` in total; degree pruning loses at most
`2p_0` in total.  At the opposite corner, the two endpoint trains together miss at
most another `2p_0` opposite trains.  Therefore the selected degree sum is at least

\[
\left[
{3S_0\over2}-(4+\sqrt2)p_0-o(1)
\right]D.
\tag{AG47.11}
\]

The atom windows shrink to axes separated by `pi/3`, so the common projection of every
selected unit train vector tends uniformly to `sqrt(3)/2`.  The §76/§163 projected
path therefore gives

\[
1\ge {\sqrt3\over2}
\left[{3S_0\over2}-(4+\sqrt2)p_0\right]-o(1).
\tag{AG47.12}
\]

For `eta=0.00047`, exact interval arithmetic gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
M_0&=1.9041557296801001\ldots,\\
R_0&=0.0009398840478144\ldots,\\
d_0&=0.0004478630897318\ldots,\\
S_0&=0.8790770859602177\ldots,\\
p_0&=0.0299286848936565\ldots,
\end{aligned}
\]

and

\[
{\sqrt3\over2}
\left[{3S_0\over2}-(4+\sqrt2)p_0\right]
>1.00162.
\tag{AG47.13}
\]

This contradicts (AG47.12).  Therefore (AG47.1) holds.

## 5. Immediate propagated floors

Every propagation in audited §§164/168 uses only the atomic gain, so at proof-only
scope one may replace the old gain by `0.00047`.  In particular,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T-q/2\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00047,
}
\tag{AG47.14}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {2f-k\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{\pi\over2}+0.00047.
}
\tag{AG47.15}
\]

For the rhombic-only train graph, the same transfer used in the explicit-rhombic note
gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\sigma_R\over D}\ge0.00094.
}
\tag{AG47.16}
\]

The correlated stopped-train identity correspondingly becomes

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R-q\over D}\ge0.00094.
}
\tag{AG47.17}
\]

Further scalar propagation is recorded separately so that this note remains focused on
the geometric proof improvement.

## 6. Scope and method ceiling

The improvement uses no new geometric hypothesis.  It only replaces a nonoptimal
phase-deletion estimate by the exact chain-graph structure already established in the
canonical endpoint proof.

A numerical root of the unchanged remaining inequalities occurs at

\[
\eta\approx0.0004764914.
\]

Thus `0.00047` is close to the ceiling of this particular proof architecture.  Further
decimal optimization of the same dominant-orbit/Mantel-pruning argument cannot produce
a macroscopic gap; a larger advance requires a new geometric ingredient.

**Disposition:** proof-only AG47 candidate awaiting independent audit. C-II remains
open.
